🔢 1. Variables in Java

A variable is a **container to store data**.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="var1"
dataType variableName = value;
```

---

📌 Examples

```java id="var2"
int age = 20;
double price = 99.99;
char grade = 'A';
boolean isActive = true;
```

---

📌 Types of Variables

🔹 Local Variable

✔ Declared inside method
✔ Scope limited to method

🔹 Instance Variable

✔ Declared inside class, outside method
✔ Belongs to object

🔹 Static Variable

✔ Declared with `static` keyword
✔ Shared among all objects

---

⚠️ Rules for Variables

✔ Must be declared before use
✔ Cannot use same name in same scope
✔ Should follow naming conventions

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔤 2. Literals in Java

Literals are **fixed values assigned to variables**.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Types of Literals

🔹 Integer Literals

```java id="lit1"
int x = 10;
int binary = 0b1010;
int hex = 0x1A;
```

---

🔹 Floating-point Literals

```java id="lit2"
float f = 3.14f;
double d = 3.14159;
```

---

🔹 Character Literals

```java id="lit3"
char ch = 'A';
char symbol = '@';
```

---

🔹 String Literals

```java id="lit4"
String name = "Java";
```

---

🔹 Boolean Literals

```java id="lit5"
boolean flag = true;
```

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚡ 3. Type Casting

Type casting is **converting one data type into another**.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 Types of Type Casting

---

1️⃣ Implicit Casting (Widening)

👉 Smaller type → larger type
✔ Done automatically

```java id="tc1"
int a = 10;
double b = a;  // int → double
```

---

2️⃣ Explicit Casting (Narrowing)

👉 Larger type → smaller type
✔ Done manually

```java id="tc2"
double x = 10.5;
int y = (int) x;  // double → int
```

---

⚠️ Data Loss Example

```java id="tc3"
double num = 9.99;
int value = (int) num;  // result = 9
```

👉 Decimal part is lost ❌

---

🎯 Why Type Casting Matters?

✔ Required for operations
✔ Helps in memory management
✔ Used in real-world programs

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Wrong casting
❌ Data loss ignored
❌ Mixing incompatible types

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Always choose correct data type and casting:

* Avoid unnecessary conversions
* Prevent runtime errors

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Variables store data
✔ Literals are fixed values
✔ Type casting converts data types
✔ Two types: implicit & explicit

